      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Southeast Asia and parts of Sub-Saharan Africa, while cultural differences play a role, the primary concerns related to labor management often stem from varying levels of:\n\n1. Training: The formal education system is sometimes inadequate, which might lead to a lack of basic manufacturing skills or understanding of processes.\n2. On-the-job training: Workers in these regions often receive limited formal training, forcing them to rely heavily on hands-on guidance to perform tasks accurately and efficiently.\n3. Industry standards: Local manufacturing practices may differ from those in more developed regions, requiring workers to be more adaptable and able to follow instructions closely.\n4. Work culture: The work ethic and expectations around punctuality, attendance, and productivity can vary, requiring more close oversight and guidance.\n\nThat being said, many factories and companies in these regions have successfully implemented effective onboarding programs, training, and quality control systems, allowing them to operate relatively independently and produce high-quality products.",
      "source": "target",
